 ## Background
-The concept of feature fields generalizes the NeRF formulation where the network predicts arbitrary per-point feature signals $\mathbf{f}$ in addition to color $\mathbf{c}$ and density $\sigma$, rather than only color [S1, unofficial]. Semantic-NeRF was notably the first to introduce semantic fields within the NeRF framework, using ground-truth semantic segmentation maps to augment the radiance field with per-point semantic logits, enabling dense 3D semantic understanding [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent works like N3F, LERF, and ISRF also learned additional feature fields to realize self-supervised 3D segmentation [S3]. With the emergence of the Segment Anything Model (SAM), it became possible to rapidly and efficiently generate object labels from text or point prompts, further advancing the field of 3D segmentation [S3].
-
-Training and exploiting feature fields introduces notable challenges. The high dimensionality of feature embeddings substantially increases memory usage and computational cost [S1, unofficial]. Moreover, the process of lifting 2D features into 3D remains fundamentally 2D-driven and lacks the intrinsic geometric reasoning that explicit 3D encoders could provide [S1, unofficial]. Finally, unlike direct NeRF interpretation methods that infer semantics in a single forward pass, feature fields require per-scene optimization before their semantics can be exploited, limiting scalability for large datasets or real-time use [S1, unofficial]. Despite these limitations, feature fields constitute a powerful middle ground between reconstruction and interpretation, transforming NeRFs into a unified representation where meaning and structure coexist [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of feature fields originated as a generalization of the NeRF formulation, allowing the prediction of arbitrary per-point feature signals rather than only color [S1, unofficial]. Semantic-NeRF was notably the first to introduce semantic fields within the NeRF framework, using ground-truth semantic segmentation maps to augment the radiance field with per-point semantic logits [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent works have expanded this to include embeddings from 2D foundation models (e.g., DINO) for self-supervised segmentation (N3F, LERF) and object-specific representations (DM-NeRF) [S2, unofficial][S3].
 
